From: Dev Patel <dev_patel@redwood.ai>
To: Natalie Green <natalie.green@clearsight.co>, Mina Haddad <mina_haddad@redwood.ai>
Date: 2028-04-02T09:18:00-07:00
Subject: Re: Redwood migration: SDK onboarding and rate-limit concerns

Hi Natalie — quick technical follow-up with a Node.js snippet you can drop into a microservice and a couple of operational knobs we recommend. Quoted Mina's checklist below and expanded on retries/concurrency.

Operational knobs:
- max_concurrency: start at 10 concurrent requests per worker for large models, raise incrementally while watching X-RateLimit-Remaining and 95th-p99 latency.
- estimated_tokens_per_request: proactively cap prompts to expected size; token estimation avoids unexpected compute spikes.
- batch window: for server-to-server batch requests, buffer 50–100ms to allow small batching without impacting latency.

Node.js sample (fetch + simple concurrency limiter + jittered backoff):
```
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const queue = require('p-queue').default;

const BASE = 'https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate';
const headers = { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' };

const limiter = new queue({ concurrency: 12 }); // start conservative

async function backoff(attempt) {
  const cap = Math.min(60000, Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100); // ms
  return Math.random() * cap;
}

async function sendRequest(payload, attempt = 0) {
  return limiter.add(async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(BASE, { method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(payload), timeout: 12000 });
      if (res.status === 200) return res.json();
      if ([429, 502, 503].includes(res.status)) {
        const wait = await backoff(attempt);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
        return sendRequest(payload, attempt + 1);
      }
      throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt < 5) {
        const wait = await backoff(attempt);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
        return sendRequest(payload, attempt + 1);
      }
      throw err;
    }
  });
}
```

Canary plan (suggested):
- Week 0: internal smoke tests + perf tests against staging replicas.
- Week 1: 5% traffic (non-critical routes) for 48–72 hours, monitor 95th/99th latency and X-RateLimit-* headers.
- Week 2: 25% traffic with expanded model set.
- Week 3: full cutover if metrics stable and no QoS regressions.

